window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-12-1", "source": "2025年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the student union of your university is collecting opinions on improving its work for the coming year. You are now to write a response by suggesting how it can better enrich student life. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "I am truly glad that the student union is collecting our opinions for the coming year, and I would like to share a\nfew thoughts on how campus life could become richer and more colourful.\nTo begin with, I suggest that more student clubs be set up, ranging from photography to debating, so that\neveryone can find a hobby and make new friends. It would also be helpful if the union organized regular sports\nmeets and art festivals, because such events relax us after study and build a stronger sense of community.\nAnother idea is to invite experts to hold lectures and workshops, which would broaden our horizons and\nprepare us for future careers.\nI believe these suggestions, if adopted, will make our daily life on campus far more enjoyable. Thank you for\nlistening, and I am always ready to offer any help the union may need."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国政府高度重视民营经济(private economy)的发展，出台了一系列政策支持民营企业。截至 2025 年 3 月底，全国民营企业数量超过 5700 万家，占企业总量的 92.3%。民营企业不仅数量稳步增长，质量和结构也在不断提升。民营企业的研发投入不断增加，在新一代信息技术、人工智能等领域发展迅速。民营企业核心竞争力的增强为中国经济的高质量发展提供了有力支撑。", "sample": "In recent years, the Chinese government has attached great importance to the development of the private economy and introduced a series of policies to support private enterprises. By the end of March 2025, the number of private enterprises nationwide exceeded 57 million, accounting for 92.3% of the total number of enterprises. Private enterprises are not only growing steadily in number but also improving in quality and structure. They are also increasing their R&D investment and developing rapidly in fields such as next-generation information technology and artificial intelligence. As a result, the enhancement of private enterprises' core competitiveness provides strong support for the high-quality development of China's economy."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2025-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "People tend to want as many choices as possible. They believe this will maximize their  __26__  of making the best decision. But research shows that, when it comes to actually making a  __27__  from all of these choices, people can become  __28__  and avoid making a decision altogether. Even worse, when people finally do decide, they are generally less satisfied with their decision and feel more regretful over whatever choice they made.\nWhy does this happen? Research shows that when people choose from many options, they  __29__  more in the decision, but feel less confident in their ability to decide well. In other words, when we are  __30__  with more choices, making the \"right\" or \"correct\" decision begins to feel more  __31__  and, at the same time, more difficult to do. This may contribute to the deep fear that we will make the wrong decision.\nHow can we solve this problem? I believe this fear could be tempered by putting decisions into perspective. It might help to remember that many of the choices you make, such as what to have for lunch, will not  __32__  much in the future and that, even more important choices, like accepting a new job, can  __33__  be changed. It could also help to  __34__  these situations with clear guidelines and ideas of what you want from the  __35__  of options, which can narrow the possible choices, and also make you more confident about your ability to make the right decision.", "matchIntro": "The battle over bike lanes needs a mindset shift", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Five years ago, the city of Queens, New York, announced that it would be putting bike lanes onto a stretch of Skillman Ave — and removing 116 parking spots. Cyclists loved the plan, but local business owners got very angry. Taking out those parking spots, as they argued at protests and in letters to the city council, would ruin stores and restaurants along Skillman. \"Parking here is already a nightmare,\" one shouted at a protest rally."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But the bike lanes were a done deal, and soon they were in place. Early this year, Jesse Coburn — an investigative writer with Streetsblog New York — wondered whether those predictions of economic collapse came true. So he asked the city's Department of Finance to give him a few years' worth of sales figures for that stretch of Skillman Ave. How had the businesses on that street fared?"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Quite well, it turns out. In the year after the bike lanes arrived, businesses on Skillman saw sales rise by 12 percent, compared to 3 percent for Queens in general. What's more, that section of road saw new businesses open, while Queens overall had a net loss."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The thing is, the actual merchants along Skillman? They didn't believe it. When Coburn spoke to them and described what he'd found, only a few store owners admitted the lanes had helped. Many still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city. And emotions ran hot: Someone scattered tacks(大头钉) on the bike lane."}, {"label": "E", "text": "This little story turns out to be a fascinating glimpse at the challenges cities face as they try to update their urban infrastructure — to clean up the air, reduce greenhouse emissions, and speed up travel by making towns more bike-friendly. There's a rising amount of data showing that installing bike lanes and making streets more pedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes of a place. Removing cars and parking spots works. But the folks who run local businesses simply aren't convinced, even when their own street performs. Given that sort of mess, can political fights over bike lanes ever end?"}, {"label": "F", "text": "In 2013, researchers at New York City's Department of Transportation studied seven stretches of road that had installed bike lanes or created pedestrian-friendly areas. The city analyzed the data for businesses along those routes and found that by the third year, sales grew faster on five of the streets than in the district overall, on average — up to five times faster, in fact."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Beyond New York, a survey of research from 23 cities found that bike lanes and pedestrian-friendly design didn't hurt local retail and food stores. (\"Fears of disastrous consequences for local businesses are unfounded,\" the researchers concluded.) More recent work has shown roughly the same."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The truth is that in fairly dense areas, bikes are more efficient at moving people around. You might lose one car driver's business — but you gain shoppers who now can arrive more easily on bikes. \"Cyclists and pedestrians are consumers too,\" notes Professor Susan Handy. Plus, streets redesigned for bikes and pedestrians tend to become more pleasant places to hang around, so \"in a lot of cases, that's created much nicer environments that are really good for those businesses.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Mom-and-pop shops are usually pretty quick at recognizing situations that will help their bottom lines. So why the blind spot here? Perhaps it's that attention focuses on horror stories — and some merchants do get hit when bike lanes come in."}, {"label": "J", "text": "I spoke to Cindy Hughes, a hairdresser(理发师) in Cambridge, Massachusetts. She said business fell by at least 40 percent when the city removed nearby parking to put in a bike lane. The majority of her customers drive, with many coming from nearby towns. Only a very few have shifted to cycling, and even those almost certainly won't cycle in Boston's snowy winters. So while Hughes supports bike lanes — \"cyclists deserve to be safe\" — she sees the parking loss as an existential risk. \"Look, 90 percent of my customers drive,\" she told me. \"For our business, the bike lanes are way worse than Covid was.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "For others, the pushback is cultural, says Henry Grabar, a writer for the Slate Magazine. Small business owners are frequently drivers who commute from other parts of the city by car, Grabar points out. They're also often longtime locals. \"They tend to be people with deep roots in the city, who have hung around since before the neighborhood became what it is today,\" he adds. Driving around town in a car is so normal to them that cycling seems weird and unusual — despite its boost from Covid, when bike sales exploded by 75 percent."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Psychology beats all! Who knew, right? The fierce divide between store owners and bike-lane advocates seems similar to our larger culture wars over climate change. If we've learned anything about culture wars, it's that data isn't much good at changing minds."}, {"label": "M", "text": "When Janette Sadik-Khan was the head of New York City's Transportation Department back in the early 2000s, she oversaw a rollout(推出) of bike lanes — and got angry blowback from residents and business owners who claimed there weren't enough cyclists to justify installing lanes. Now, she notes ironically, the lanes are so full of activity that opponents have turned to claiming the problem is the opposite: There are too many cyclists getting in the way of cars."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Maybe bike lanes will always be charged with emotion, until enough of the public is finally concerned about climate change — and it seems reckless(鲁莽的) to not have them."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Crises, after all, have a way of opening people's eyes to possibilities. During Covid, restaurants and cafés lost so much business that cities nationwide began allowing them to build curbside seating areas where people could sit, safely, in the open air. It greatly reduced parking — but because, well, shop owners didn't see any way around it. Customers loved the outdoor seating so much that cities are making it permanent: A New York City study of several streets closed during Covid found storeowners making more than before, and diners enjoying the outdoor lifestyle. If data won't change minds, customers might."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "All living organisms on Earth are exposed to a 24-hour day-night cycle. This cycle is the reason why people rest at night and are active during the day. Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of behaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health. For example, eating at night can lead to weight gain over time because food intake at night leads to increased fat storage.\nMany drug targets in the body follow a 24-hour cycle, too. This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period. Because how the body responds to a drug can differ depending on the time it is taken, it logically follows that taking medicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce unwanted side effects.\nWhen doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it. There are two main reasons for that oversight. First, many physicians are not aware that some drugs work better during a specific time of the day. And second, most drugs have not been studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle. Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure compliance.\nOver 50 years ago, researchers found that the cholesterol drug simvastatin is more effective at lowering cholesterol levels when taken at night rather than during the day. This is because the liver enzyme(酶) these drugs target is more active at night.\nTaking medicine at the wrong time can even cause harm. My colleagues and I wondered whether midazolam, the most common sedative(镇静剂) used in surgical procedures worldwide, might interfere with the internal clock that protects the heart at night. Currently, there are no guidelines regarding when midazolam should be administered.\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different diseases. I believe taking drug timing into account could help make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide.", "47": "All living organisms on Earth are exposed to a 24-hour day-night cycle. This cycle is the reason why people rest at night and are active during the day. Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of behaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health. For example, eating at night can lead to weight gain over time because food intake at night leads to increased fat storage.\nMany drug targets in the body follow a 24-hour cycle, too. This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period. Because how the body responds to a drug can differ depending on the time it is taken, it logically follows that taking medicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce unwanted side effects.\nWhen doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it. There are two main reasons for that oversight. First, many physicians are not aware that some drugs work better during a specific time of the day. And second, most drugs have not been studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle. Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure compliance.\nOver 50 years ago, researchers found that the cholesterol drug simvastatin is more effective at lowering cholesterol levels when taken at night rather than during the day. This is because the liver enzyme(酶) these drugs target is more active at night.\nTaking medicine at the wrong time can even cause harm. My colleagues and I wondered whether midazolam, the most common sedative(镇静剂) used in surgical procedures worldwide, might interfere with the internal clock that protects the heart at night. Currently, there are no guidelines regarding when midazolam should be administered.\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different diseases. I believe taking drug timing into account could help make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide.", "48": "All living organisms on Earth are exposed to a 24-hour day-night cycle. This cycle is the reason why people rest at night and are active during the day. Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of behaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health. For example, eating at night can lead to weight gain over time because food intake at night leads to increased fat storage.\nMany drug targets in the body follow a 24-hour cycle, too. This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period. Because how the body responds to a drug can differ depending on the time it is taken, it logically follows that taking medicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce unwanted side effects.\nWhen doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it. There are two main reasons for that oversight. First, many physicians are not aware that some drugs work better during a specific time of the day. And second, most drugs have not been studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle. Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure compliance.\nOver 50 years ago, researchers found that the cholesterol drug simvastatin is more effective at lowering cholesterol levels when taken at night rather than during the day. This is because the liver enzyme(酶) these drugs target is more active at night.\nTaking medicine at the wrong time can even cause harm. My colleagues and I wondered whether midazolam, the most common sedative(镇静剂) used in surgical procedures worldwide, might interfere with the internal clock that protects the heart at night. Currently, there are no guidelines regarding when midazolam should be administered.\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different diseases. I believe taking drug timing into account could help make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide.", "49": "All living organisms on Earth are exposed to a 24-hour day-night cycle. This cycle is the reason why people rest at night and are active during the day. Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of behaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health. For example, eating at night can lead to weight gain over time because food intake at night leads to increased fat storage.\nMany drug targets in the body follow a 24-hour cycle, too. This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period. Because how the body responds to a drug can differ depending on the time it is taken, it logically follows that taking medicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce unwanted side effects.\nWhen doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it. There are two main reasons for that oversight. First, many physicians are not aware that some drugs work better during a specific time of the day. And second, most drugs have not been studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle. Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure compliance.\nOver 50 years ago, researchers found that the cholesterol drug simvastatin is more effective at lowering cholesterol levels when taken at night rather than during the day. This is because the liver enzyme(酶) these drugs target is more active at night.\nTaking medicine at the wrong time can even cause harm. My colleagues and I wondered whether midazolam, the most common sedative(镇静剂) used in surgical procedures worldwide, might interfere with the internal clock that protects the heart at night. Currently, there are no guidelines regarding when midazolam should be administered.\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different diseases. I believe taking drug timing into account could help make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide.", "50": "All living organisms on Earth are exposed to a 24-hour day-night cycle. This cycle is the reason why people rest at night and are active during the day. Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of behaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health. For example, eating at night can lead to weight gain over time because food intake at night leads to increased fat storage.\nMany drug targets in the body follow a 24-hour cycle, too. This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period. Because how the body responds to a drug can differ depending on the time it is taken, it logically follows that taking medicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce unwanted side effects.\nWhen doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it. There are two main reasons for that oversight. First, many physicians are not aware that some drugs work better during a specific time of the day. And second, most drugs have not been studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle. Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure compliance.\nOver 50 years ago, researchers found that the cholesterol drug simvastatin is more effective at lowering cholesterol levels when taken at night rather than during the day. This is because the liver enzyme(酶) these drugs target is more active at night.\nTaking medicine at the wrong time can even cause harm. My colleagues and I wondered whether midazolam, the most common sedative(镇静剂) used in surgical procedures worldwide, might interfere with the internal clock that protects the heart at night. Currently, there are no guidelines regarding when midazolam should be administered.\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different diseases. I believe taking drug timing into account could help make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide.", "51": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser(理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\nBoth scenarios(情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.\nIn addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.\n\"Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you're doing,\" Abraham says.\nThe overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\nMartin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They're also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.\nWorkers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.", "52": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser(理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\nBoth scenarios(情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.\nIn addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.\n\"Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you're doing,\" Abraham says.\nThe overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\nMartin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They're also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.\nWorkers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.", "53": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser(理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\nBoth scenarios(情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.\nIn addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.\n\"Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you're doing,\" Abraham says.\nThe overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\nMartin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They're also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.\nWorkers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.", "54": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser(理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\nBoth scenarios(情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.\nIn addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.\n\"Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you're doing,\" Abraham says.\nThe overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\nMartin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They're also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.\nWorkers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.", "55": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser(理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\nBoth scenarios(情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.\nIn addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.\n\"Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you're doing,\" Abraham says.\nThe overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\nMartin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They're also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.\nWorkers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Where was the black cat found?", "options": ["A) Outside the office of a charity foundation.", "B) Inside the car of David King's neighbour.", "C) Under the engine cover of a man's car.", "D) At the gate of a grade school in Kent."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Under the engine cover of a man's car.\n【定位】The black cat was found curled up under the engine cover of David King's car when he\ndecided to do an oil check after dropping his grandson off at school in Kent.\n这只黑猫是在大卫·金送孙子上学后、决定检查机油时，被发现蜷缩在他汽车的引擎盖下\n面的。\n【信号】found ＋ 地点介词短语。答案句的信号是动词 found 紧跟一个表地点的介词短语 found\ncurled up under the engine cover of David King's car——题干问 Where… found，原\n文恰好出现 found ＋『在哪儿』，这就是承载答案的那一句。\n【替换】under the engine cover of David King's car ↔ Under the engine cover of a man's car.\n人名换成泛称 a man\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项把后文出现的 UK charity Cats Protection 里的 charity 一词搬来，编成『慈善机构门\n口』这个地点。可录音里 charity 只用来说明谁救的猫，从没说猫是在慈善机构门口被发现\n的，属听到 charity 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Following a rescue by UK charity Cats Protection\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项把末段的 Mr King's neighbour（邻居）搬来，说猫是在『邻居的车里』被发现。但邻\n居在录音里是猫的主人、出现在结局处，发现猫的车是金先生自己的车，把发现地点安到邻\n居车上是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：reunited with its owner, Mr King's neighbour\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项抓住原文的 school in Kent，把『学校门口』说成发现地点。可录音里 school 是金先\n生送孙子去的地方、是事情发生前的背景，猫是他送完孙子、回来做机油检查时才在引擎盖\n下被发现的，发现地不是学校门口。\n借用原文：after dropping his grandson off at school in Kent", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the cat at the end of the news report?", "options": ["A) It got reunited with its owner.", "B) It was injured during the rescue.", "C) It became a pet of Mr King's grandson.", "D) It was placed in the care of a UK charity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It got reunited with its owner.\n【定位】Following a rescue by UK charity Cats Protection, the four-year-old cat was later reunited\nwith its owner, Mr King's neighbour.\n在英国慈善机构『猫咪保护』救援之后，这只四岁的猫最终与它的主人——金先生的邻居\n——团聚了。\n【信号】末段＋later标结局。本句是录音最末一段、且含时间词 later，正对应题干的 at the end；\n新闻的结果通常落在最后一句，later reunited 直接交代了猫的最终下场，是答案句。\n【替换】reunited with its owner ↔ It got reunited with its owner.\n近乎原句的同义重述\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项说猫『在救援中受伤』，但录音从头到尾只字未提受伤，末段只说被救后团聚。它利用\n第3段 terrified（受惊）、stuck（卡住）等词让人联想到猫很惨，进而脑补成『受伤』，实\n属过度推断、原文未提。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项把第1段的 grandson（孙子）搬来，说猫成了金先生孙子的宠物。可孙子只在『送孙\n子上学』的背景里出现，录音末段明说猫团聚的对象是主人即邻居，不是被孙子养，属张冠\n李戴。\n借用原文：after dropping his grandson off at school in Kent\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项说猫『被交由英国一家慈善机构照管』，盗用了末段 UK charity Cats Protection 这个\n真出现的机构。但录音里 charity 是负责『救援』的一方，救完之后猫是团聚而非留在机构\n照管，把过程中的救援者说成最终归宿，差在结局。\n借用原文：Following a rescue by UK charity Cats Protection\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 In less than a month, the Special Olympics Spring Games will make a return to Fayetteville.\nThe Games are coming back for the first time in five years. The event will take place at\nMethodist University.\n不到一个月后,特殊奥林匹克春季运动会将重返Fayetteville。这是该运动会五年来首次回归。活动将在\nMethodist University举行。\n2 Event organizer Benjamin Kowalczyk says he's excited that athletes will get a chance to come back\nand demonstrate their abilities. Organizers expect about 100 athletes will come out to compete in\nFayetteville. They will have a variety of events to choose from, including running, throwing, and\njumping. There will also be a fun tent for children. Kowalczyk said it's rewarding to see athletes with\nspecial needs triumph in the Games.\n活动组织者Benjamin Kowalczyk表示,他很高兴运动员们将有机会回来展示他们的能力。组织者预计约有\n100名运动员将来到Fayetteville参加比赛。他们将有多种项目可供选择,包括跑步、投掷和跳跃。现场还\n将设有儿童趣味帐篷。Kowalczyk说,看到有特殊需求的运动员在运动会上取得胜利是很有意义的。\n3 For anyone who wants to help make this year's Games a massive success, there are still opportunities\nto volunteer for Fayetteville's Special Olympics. Organizers encourage them to visit the Games' website\nto sign up.\n对于任何想要帮助今年运动会取得巨大成功的人,仍有机会为Fayetteville特殊奥林匹克运动会做志愿者。\n组织者鼓励他们访问运动会网站进行报名。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What will Fayetteville witness in less than a month?", "options": ["A) The 100th anniversary celebration of Methodist University.", "B) The first important political event in the next five years.", "C) The opening of the Special Olympics Spring Games.", "D) The reunion of this year's Olympic gold medalists."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The opening of the Special Olympics Spring Games.\n【定位】In less than a month, the Special Olympics Spring Games will make a return to Fayetteville.\n不到一个月后，特殊奥林匹克运动会春季赛事将重返费耶特维尔。\n【信号】题干时间词原样出现。录音开头 In less than a month 与题干 in less than a month 用词\n完全一致，这种\"题干时间状语在原文原样响起\"就是最强的定位信号，听到它，紧跟其后的\n主语就是答案所在。\n【替换】the Special Olympics Spring Games will make a return ↔\nThe opening of the Special Olympics Spring Games.\n重返即开赛\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到了 Methodist University（卫理公会大学），A 就把这个原词捡来编出 The\n100th anniversary celebration of Methodist University.\"百年校庆\"，但原文只说赛事在\n该校举办，从没提任何校庆或\"100th\"，是典型听到地名就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The event will take place at Methodist University.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说赛事是 coming back for the first time in five years（五年来首次回归），B 把\n\"five years\" 这个原词改头换面成 The first important political event in the next five\nyears.，既把\"赛事\"偷换成\"政治大事\"，又把\"过去五年\"扭成\"未来五年\"，是盗用数字+偷换\n概念的双重陷阱。\n借用原文：The Games are coming back for the first time in five years.\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 The reunion of this year's Olympic gold medalists. 借了原文反复出现的\n\"Olympics\" 来误导：原文是 Special Olympics（特殊奥林匹克/残障人士运动会），D 偷\n偷掐掉 \"Special\" 变成普通\"Olympic gold medalists 金牌选手聚会\"，原文从未提金牌选手\n聚会，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：the Special Olympics Spring Games will make a return to Fayetteville", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did Benjamin Kowalczyk say is rewarding to see?", "options": ["A) Volunteers visit the Games' website to sign up.", "B) Athletes with special needs triumph in the Games.", "C) Organizers devote their time and energy to the Games.", "D) Children play in a fun tent and enjoy themselves fully."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Athletes with special needs triumph in the Games.\n【定位】Kowalczyk said it's rewarding to see athletes with special needs triumph in the Games.\nKowalczyk 说，看到有特殊需求的运动员在赛事中取得胜利，是很有意义、很值得的。\n【信号】题干rewarding原话再现。录音 Kowalczyk said it's rewarding to see 把题干里的人名和\n评价词 \"rewarding\" 原样念出，这是\"人物+评价词\"双重信号，听到它就知道接下来 \"to\nsee\" 后面那件事正是答案承载句。\n【替换】athletes with special needs triumph in the Games ↔\nAthletes with special needs triumph in the Games.\n评价词后内容直接对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第三段确有 visit the Games' website to sign up，A 把这句原词整段搬来，但那是\n给\"想当志愿者的人\"的报名指引，不是 Kowalczyk \"觉得值得一看\"的事，盗用原词却答非所\n问。\n借用原文：Organizers encourage them to visit the Games' website to sign up.\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 Organizers devote their time and energy to the Games. 看似贴合主题，但原文\nKowalczyk 评\"值得\"的是运动员取胜，并没说\"组织者投入时间精力\"这件事值得看；原文只\n说想帮忙的人可去志愿，把评价对象悄悄换成了组织者，属张冠李戴+未提及。\n借用原文：it's rewarding to see athletes with special needs triumph in the Games\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第二段确实念了 There will also be a fun tent for children.，D 把 \"fun\ntent/children\" 这组原词捡来，但那只是顺带交代赛事设施，Kowalczyk 的 \"rewarding\"\n评价根本没落在孩子玩耍上，是听到原词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：There will also be a fun tent for children.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What has the German supermarket been ordered to do?", "options": ["A) Destroy its imitation chocolate rabbits.", "B) Change the wrapping of its commodities.", "C) Compensate for the Swiss manufacturer's loss.", "D) Defend itself in the country's commercial court."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Destroy its imitation chocolate rabbits.\n【定位】A German supermarket has been ordered to destroy its chocolate rabbits after it lost a court\nbattle with a Swiss chocolate manufacturer.\n一家德国超市在与一家瑞士巧克力制造商的官司中败诉后，被勒令销毁其巧克力兔子。\n【信号】命令信号 ordered to。答案句的信号是 has been ordered to destroy，ordered to 正是\n题干 been ordered to 的呼应，这种表命令、判决的短语就是答案落点，听到立刻锁定其\n后的动作。\n【替换】destroy its chocolate rabbits ↔ Destroy its imitation chocolate rabbits.\n近乎同表述加补词\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Change the wrapping of its commodities. 借用了原文后文反复提到的 wrapper\n（包装），但原文从没要求超市改包装，命令是销毁，属听到 wrapping 就想选的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：The colour of the wrapper is similar\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Compensate for the Swiss manufacturer's loss. 拼用了原文的 Swiss manufacturer\n和 lost（败诉），但原文判的是销毁，从未提赔偿损失，是把熟词重组出的假动作。\n借用原文：it lost a court battle with a Swiss chocolate manufacturer\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Defend itself in the country's commercial court. 借了原文的 commercial court，\n但那是去年商业法院的旧判决场景，本题命令是销毁兔子，并非要超市去出庭辩护。\n借用原文：overturned a ruling last year by the country's commercial court", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did Switzerland's highest court suggest about the chocolate in question?", "options": ["A) It could be rewrapped and sold by the budget supermarket.", "B) It could be reshaped into animals other than rabbits.", "C) It could be resold cheaper to avoid waste.", "D) It could be reused in other products."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It could be reused in other products.\n【定位】It ordered that all the imitation rabbits be destroyed, but suggested the chocolate needn't be\nwasted. It could be melted for use in other products.\n法院下令销毁所有仿制兔子，但建议巧克力不必浪费，可以熔化后用于其他产品。\n【信号】转折 but 后是真意。答案句信号是转折词 but suggested the chocolate needn't be\nwasted，but 前讲销毁、but 后才是对巧克力的建议，转折之后才是落点，这正呼应题干\n的 suggest。\n【替换】melted for use in other products ↔ It could be reused in other products.\n熔化再用即重复使用\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 rewrapped and sold by the budget supermarket 搬来原文的 budget\nsupermarket，但原文建议是熔化再用，从没说重新包装由该超市出售，是熟词拼出的假处\n置。\n借用原文：a similar product sold by the budget supermarket\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 reshaped into animals other than rabbits 借了原文 rabbits 一词臆造出改造成别的\n动物，原文只说熔化用于其他产品，并未提改塑成动物。\n借用原文：all the imitation rabbits be destroyed\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 resold cheaper to avoid waste 借了原文 needn't be wasted 中的 waste 概念，但\n原文是熔化再用以避免浪费，不是降价转售，处置动作被偷换。\n借用原文：the chocolate needn't be wasted", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why did Switzerland's highest court overturn the commercial court's ruling?", "options": ["A) To be fair to the German supermarkets.", "B) To protect chocolate retailers' interests.", "C) To boost the growth of the chocolate industry.", "D) To prevent consumers' possible confusion."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To prevent consumers' possible confusion.\n【定位】It said even though there are some differences between the two products, there was still the\npossibility of confusion for consumers.\n法院表示，尽管两种产品之间存在一些差异，但仍有可能让消费者产生混淆。\n【信号】让步转折露真因。答案句信号是让步结构 even though there are some\ndifferences...there was still the possibility of confusion，even though 让一步、\nbut/still 后才是真正理由——担心消费者混淆，这就是推翻判决的原因。\n【替换】the possibility of confusion for consumers ↔ To prevent consumers' possible confusion.\n存在混淆即为防混淆\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 To be fair to the German supermarkets. 借了原文反复出现的 German\nsupermarket，但原文推翻原判恰恰对超市不利，目的是防混淆而非对超市公平，方向相\n反。\n借用原文：A German supermarket has been ordered to destroy\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 To protect chocolate retailers' interests. 把原文的 consumers（消费者）偷换成\nretailers（零售商），原文明说担心的是消费者混淆，保护对象被换了一方。\n借用原文：the possibility of confusion for consumers\nC) 未提及\n选项 To boost the growth of the chocolate industry. 借了反复出现的 chocolate，但原\n文从未提推动巧克力行业发展这一目的，是凭熟词臆造的目的。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man request the woman to do?", "options": ["A) Return the handbook to him.", "B) Make a vegetable dish.", "C) Pass a book to him.", "D) Meet his sister's boyfriend."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Pass a book to him.\n【定位】Can you please hand me that book over there?\n你能把那边那本书递给我吗？\n【信号】请求句 Can you please...。答案句的信号是请求口吻 Can you please hand me that\nbook over there?，Can you please 一出，后面跟的动作 hand me that book 就是男的\n请女的做的事，这正是\"做什么\"题要锁定的落点。\n【替换】hand me that book ↔ Pass a book to him.\nhand 与 pass 同义换词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 Return the handbook to him. 用 handbook 蹭录音里的 hand me that book 的发\n音（hand...book），听着像，但录音说的是 hand（递）+ book（书），不是 handbook\n（手册），更没有 return（还）的意思。\n借用原文：hand me that book\nB) 答非所问\nB 的 Make a vegetable dish. 用了录音 making a winter bean salad 里的做素菜原词，但\n做菜是男的自己\"I need to make\"，不是请女的做，答非所问。\n借用原文：I need to make a lot of vegetable dishes\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 Meet his sister's boyfriend. 取自录音 My sister's boyfriend is coming over for\ndinner 的原词，但那只是交代背景（男友要来吃饭），并非男的请女的去见他。\n借用原文：My sister's boyfriend is coming over for dinner", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say about eating vegetables only?", "options": ["A) It doesn't seem to be a balanced diet.", "B) It would reduce one's protein intake.", "C) It would do harm to one's muscles.", "D) It doesn't sound practical for her."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It doesn't seem to be a balanced diet.\n【定位】He only eats vegetables, no meat? That doesn't sound like a very balanced diet.\n他只吃菜、不吃肉？那听起来不像是很均衡的饮食啊。\n【信号】评价句 That doesn't sound like...。答案信号是女的抛出的总评价 That doesn't sound\nlike a very balanced diet，doesn't sound like 是直接表态，这是她对\"只吃素\"的核心判\n断，后面 protein、muscles 都是她为这个判断补的理由。\n【替换】That doesn't sound like a very balanced diet ↔ It doesn't seem to be a balanced diet.\nsound like 换 seem to be\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 It would reduce one's protein intake. 蹭了录音 How can he get enough protein?\n的 protein 原词，但女的只是反问\"怎么获取足够蛋白质\"来支撑她的判断，并没下\"会减少蛋\n白质摄入\"的结论。\n借用原文：How can he get enough protein?\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 It would do harm to one's muscles. 借用录音 strengthen his muscles 的 muscles\n原词，但女的是问\"他怎么练肌肉\"，并没说\"会伤肌肉\"，属于无中生有地拔高。\n借用原文：strengthen his muscles\nD) 偷换概念\nD 的 It doesn't sound practical for her. 套用了录音 sound 的口吻，但女的评价的是\"这\n饮食不均衡\"，从没说\"对她本人不实际\"，把评判对象悄悄换成了\"对她\"。\n借用原文：That doesn't sound like a very balanced diet", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why does the boyfriend of the man's sister choose to be a vegetarian?", "options": ["A) To improve his health.", "B) To protect animal rights.", "C) To follow a trendy lifestyle.", "D) To stick to his religious belief."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To protect animal rights.\n【定位】He's an animal activist. He's always been very sensitive and sympathizes with animals.\n他是个动物保护活动者。他一向很敏感，对动物充满同情。\n【信号】亮明身份句 He's an animal activist。答案信号是男的直接亮出男友的身份与立场 He's an\nanimal activist，紧接 sympathizes with animals。原因题里这种\"He's a + 身份/立场\"的\n句子，往往就是动机所在——他吃素是因为他是动物保护者。\n【替换】He's an animal activist ↔ To protect animal rights.\n身份具体说法换成概括目的\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 To improve his health. 蹭了录音 health condition 的 health 原词，但那是女的列举\n可能原因时随口提的选项之一，录音随后明确否掉、给出的真实原因是动物保护，不是健\n康。\n借用原文：Is it his religion, health condition, or lifestyle?\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 To follow a trendy lifestyle. 借用录音 lifestyle 原词，但这同样只是女的列举的待选\n项之一，男的回答里根本没提\"赶潮流\"，是答非所问。\n借用原文：Is it his religion, health condition, or lifestyle?\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 To stick to his religious belief. 用了录音 religion 原词，且\"宗教吃素\"最符合日常常\n识，最易被默认选中；但录音明说原因是动物保护，宗教只是女的列举的可能性之一，并非\n答案。\n借用原文：Is it his religion, health condition, or lifestyle?", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "On what point does the man agree with the woman at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) The torture and distress dolphins suffer in parks.", "B) The necessity of having zoos and eating meat.", "C) The gloomy environments animals are kept in.", "D) The urgency of joining an animal rights group."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The necessity of having zoos and eating meat.\n【定位】W: Not all zoos and animal parks have the most favorable conditions, but without them it just\nwouldn't be feasible to learn about animals and their environments. Besides, I don't think I\ncould ever give up a good hot dog at a baseball game. M: Honestly, I agree.\n女：并不是所有动物园和动物公园条件都最理想，但要是没有它们，就没法了解动物和它们\n的生存环境了。再说，我可舍不得放弃看棒球赛时来一根热狗。男：说实话，我同意。\n【信号】赞同信号 Honestly, I agree。答案信号是男的结尾的明确赞同 Honestly, I agree，它指向\n的是女的紧挨着说的两点——动物园有存在的必要 without them it just wouldn't be\nfeasible to learn about animals 和不愿放弃吃热狗（肉）I could ever give up a good\nhot dog。赞同句认同的就是这两点合起来的立场。\n【替换】without them it just wouldn't be feasible to learn about animals ↔\nThe necessity of having zoos and eating meat.\n没了就不行 = 有必要\nI don't think I could ever give up a good hot dog ↔ eating meat\n舍不得放弃热狗 = 要吃肉\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 的 The torture and distress dolphins suffer in parks. 几乎照搬录音 the torture of\ndolphins in sea parks 与 causes them great distress 的原词，但那是男友（动物活动\n家）的立场，不是男女二人结尾达成的共识，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the torture of dolphins in sea parks\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 The gloomy environments animals are kept in. 蹭录音 That's a little gloomy 的\ngloomy 原词，但 gloomy 是女的形容男友说法\"有点令人沮丧\"，并非两人一致认同\"动物\n处境阴郁\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：That's a little gloomy\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 The urgency of joining an animal rights group. 借用录音 his local animal rights\ngroup 的 animal rights group 原词，但录音只是说男友是该组织发言人，根本没提\"加入\n组织的紧迫性\"，属凭空编造。\n借用原文：He's the spokesperson for his local animal rights group", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What part of the TV program does the man say was interesting?", "options": ["A) The part about getting over jetlag.", "B) The part about air travel in the past.", "C) The part about not eating on board the plane.", "D) The part about avoiding sleeping on the plane."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The part about getting over jetlag.\n【定位】The part about overcoming jetlag was interesting.\n关于克服时差的那一部分很有趣。\n【信号】被问者男士的直接应答 + 评价词 interesting。本题问的是男士，男士开口的 The part\nabout overcoming jetlag was interesting. 就是被问者的直接应答，句中 interesting 与\n题干 interesting 完全对应，是把这句锁成答案句的信号。\n【替换】overcoming jetlag ↔ getting over jetlag\n同义短语替换 overcome=get over\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThe part about air travel in the past 里 air travel 是整段对话的大话题词，听着耳熟，但\n男士说有趣的是克服时差那部分，\"过去的航空旅行\"录音并未作为有趣的一部分提到，属拿\n大话题词凑出的诱选。\nC) 原词陷阱\nnot eating on board the plane 盗用了录音 not eating for the entire journey 这个原词\n内容，但那是男士转述\"专家推荐\"的建议、且他说的是\"surprised（吃惊）\"，并非回答\"哪\n部分有趣\"。听到 not eating 就选即落陷阱。\n借用原文：the expert recommended not eating for the entire journey\nD) 原词陷阱\navoiding sleeping on the plane 盗用录音 avoiding sleeping on the plane 的原词，但\n这与 C 一样属男士转述的专家建议、令他吃惊的内容，并不是他点名\"有趣\"的那部分（有趣\n的是克服时差整块），属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：avoiding sleeping on the plane", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the man say he will do next time he flies a long distance?", "options": ["A) Take a nap to adjust to a new time zone.", "B) Have meals as usual to stay away from hunger.", "C) Concentrate on reading articles recommended by experts.", "D) Try following the advice given by the expert on the show."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Try following the advice given by the expert on the show.\n【定位】the expert on the show did cite research supporting her recommendations, so I guess I'll give\nit a try next time I fly a long distance.\n节目里的那位专家确实引用了支持她建议的研究，所以我想下次长途飞行时我会试试看。\n【信号】表打算的信号 I'll give it a try + 时间短语 next time。I guess I'll give it a try next time\nI fly a long distance 里的 I'll give it a try 是典型\"打算\"信号，又带 next time I fly a long\ndistance 与题干时间完全吻合，是锁定答案句的双重信号。\n【替换】give it a try ↔ Try following the advice\ngive it a try=try following，照建议试一试\nthe expert on the show did cite research supporting her recommendations ↔\nthe advice given by the expert on the show\nher recommendations=the advice，同为专家建议\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nTake a nap to adjust to a new time zone 盗用了女士那句 taking a nap on the plane\nwas the best way to adjust to a new time zone 的原词，但那是女士转述\"过去文章\"的主\n张，且专家恰恰建议\"不要睡\"，并非男士下次要做的事，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：taking a nap on the plane was the best way to adjust to a new time zone\nB) 张冠李戴\nHave meals as usual to stay away from hunger 对应女士读到的旧观点 it was\nimportant not to miss meals，而男士打算照的是\"专家建议\"，专家主张是 not eating\n——男士要做的恰与 B 相反，属张冠李戴兼与原文相反。\n借用原文：it was important not to miss meals\nC) 偷换概念\nConcentrate on reading articles recommended by experts 把录音里的 expert 与 W 提\n到的 an article on the subject 拼在一起，造出\"读专家推荐的文章\"，但男士说的是 give it\na try（照建议试做），并非去读文章，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：I read an article on the subject in the past", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why does the woman think she is lucky?", "options": ["A) She does not have any problem flying.", "B) She can spend a lot of time vacationing.", "C) She does not have to worry about jetlag.", "D) She has been well treated when traveling."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She does not have to worry about jetlag.\n【定位】she did say that jetlag often becomes more of a problem after 40, so I guess I'm lucky that I\ncan still adjust to different time zones well.\n她确实说过时差在 40 岁以后往往会变成更大的问题，所以我想我算幸运，因为我还能很好\n地适应不同的时区。\n【信号】标志词 I'm lucky + that 引出理由。I'm lucky that I can still adjust to different time\nzones well 里 I'm lucky 直接对应题干 lucky，紧跟的 that 从句给出理由——这个\"幸运 +\nthat 理由\"结构就是答案句信号。\n【替换】I can still adjust to different time zones well ↔ She does not have to worry about jetlag\n能适应时区=不必担心时差，肯否定转换\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShe does not have any problem flying 与原文相反：女士紧接着说 My problem is I'm\nafraid to fly，明说自己有怕飞的问题，所以\"飞行无问题\"是反着说的，且她的幸运也不是指\n这点。\n借用原文：My problem is I'm afraid to fly\nB) 未提及\nShe can spend a lot of time vacationing 里 vacation 蹭了后文男士提到的 our family\nvacations，但那是男士讲自家度假方式，女士的幸运与\"能度很多假\"毫无关系，属未提及。\n借用原文：our family vacations were always by car or train\nD) 未提及\nShe has been well treated when traveling\"旅行中受优待\"在整段录音里没有任何对应内\n容，与女士的幸运（能适应时区）也不搭，属凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "Why does the woman think the problem of being afraid to fly deserves attention?", "options": ["A) It has caused heavy losses to many airlines.", "B) It impacts female travelers more seriously.", "C) It has long been ignored by many experts.", "D) It affects twenty-five percent of people."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It affects twenty-five percent of people.\n【定位】Actually, it was a quarter of people, so the problem really is widespread and\ndeserves attention.\n其实是四分之一的人，所以这个问题确实很普遍，值得关注。\n【信号】纠正信号 Actually + 数字 a quarter，且与题干 deserves attention 同词。女士用\nActually, it was a quarter of people 纠正男士先前说的数字，so the problem really is\nwidespread and deserves attention 里 deserves attention 与题干同词——Actually 的\n纠正语气与同词回扣是双重信号。\n【替换】a quarter of people ↔ twenty-five percent of people\na quarter=25%，分数与百分比换算\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt has caused heavy losses to many airlines 蹭了全篇的 air travel/航空话题，但\"给航空\n公司造成巨额亏损\"录音从未提及，女士给的理由是比例之高，不是公司亏损，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nIt impacts female travelers more seriously 凭空加了\"female travelers、more\nseriously\"的范围与程度，录音只说影响四分之一的人、并未区分性别，属无中生有地偷加\n范围程度。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt has long been ignored by many experts 借了对话反复出现的 expert 设原词诱惑，但\n录音说的是这问题 deserves attention（值得关注），并未说\"长期被专家忽视\"，方向相\n反，属与原文相反兼借词诱选。\n借用原文：deserves attention", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker think is the best way to get started in user experience design?", "options": ["A) Learning from skilled designers.", "B) Obtaining real-world experience.", "C) Joining a 6-month training camp.", "D) Anticipating actual challenges."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Obtaining real-world experience.\n【定位】The most common question I'm asked always centers around how to get started in user\nexperience design, to which my response is always that nothing can substitute real-\nworld experience.\n别人最常问我的问题总是围绕怎么入门用户体验设计，对此我的回答始终是：没有什么能替\n代真实世界的经验。\n【信号】nothing can substitute抬高某项。答案信号是这句里的 nothing can substitute real-\nworld experience（没有什么能替代真实世界的经验）——nothing can substitute 是把\n某一项捧到唯一、最高位置的强调说法，凡听到这种把别的全比下去的句式，那句就是答\n案句。\n【替换】nothing can substitute real-world experience ↔ Obtaining real-world experience.\n唯一不可替代等于最佳\n【排除】A) 未提及\nLearning from skilled designers. 听起来很合理，但录音通篇没说过要向熟练的设计师\n学，它是凭常识脑补的入门方式，原文无对应。\nC) 原词陷阱\nJoining a 6-month training camp. 盗用了录音第三段的原词 6-month training camp，\n但原文是把训练营当成学不到适应力的反面例子来说的，并非入门的最佳方式，属于听到原\n词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：much less in a 6-month training camp\nD) 原词陷阱\nAnticipating actual challenges. 借用了第二段原词 actual challenges，但原文说的是高等\n教育无法让你为真实挑战做好准备，并没说入门的最佳方式是去预判挑战，选项把原词搬过\n来又改了意思。\n借用原文：it will not prepare you for actual challenges you'll face", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker say being effective requires?", "options": ["A) Core skills.", "B) Higher education.", "C) Adaptability.", "D) Capability."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Adaptability.\n【定位】Being effective requires adaptability — something you don't really learn in school, much less\nin a 6-month training camp.\n要做到有效就需要适应力——这是你在学校里学不太到的东西，在六个月的训练营里就更别\n提了。\n【信号】requires直接点出条件。答案信号就是题干原话在录音里几乎复现：Being effective\nrequires adaptability，requires 后面紧跟的 adaptability 就是被点名的条件。这种题干和\n原文几乎同形、只差答案词的句子，是最直接的答案句信号。\n【替换】Being effective requires adaptability ↔ Adaptability.\n原词同形直接对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nCore skills. 盗用了第二段原词 core skills，但原文说的是高等教育能帮你装备一些核心技\n能、随后却话锋一转说它不够，core skills 并不是 being effective 所需要的那样东西，属\n听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Higher education is a great way to equip yourself with some core skills\nB) 原词陷阱\nHigher education. 盗用第二段原词 Higher education，但原文说高等教育不能让你应对\n真实挑战、是被往下压的一项，根本不是 being effective 所需，听到这个原词就选会掉\n坑。\n借用原文：but it will not prepare you for actual challenges\nD) 未提及\nCapability. 和正确项 Adaptability. 一样以 -ability 结尾、长得像，是专为只听个相似尾音\n的考生设的形近干扰，但录音从没说 being effective 需要 capability。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What can people do as long as they know their end goal and strive for it?", "options": ["A) Start anywhere.", "B) Face challenges.", "C) Pursue roles in teams.", "D) Follow their own path."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Start anywhere.\n【定位】You can start anywhere as long as you know your end goal and you commit to actively\npursuing opportunities to learn and grow along the way.\n只要你清楚自己的终极目标，并且愿意一路上主动去追寻学习和成长的机会，你可以从任何\n地方起步。\n【信号】as long as条件句的主句。答案信号是这句的结构 You can start anywhere as long as\nyou know your end goal——as long as 是题干照搬的条件信号词，它前面的主句 You\ncan start anywhere 就是回答能做什么的部分，条件句的主句才是答案落点。\n【替换】You can start anywhere ↔ Start anywhere.\n主句原话浓缩为动词短语\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nFace challenges. 里的 challenges 来自前文（如第二段说的 actual challenges、第四段说\n团队能 challenge me），但 as long as 那句答案句里说的是 start anywhere，不是面对\n挑战，选项搬了别处的原词来凑。\n借用原文：roles within teams that I knew could teach and challenge me\nC) 张冠李戴\nPursue roles in teams. 盗用第四段原词 pursue roles within teams，但那是作者讲自己当\n年的经历（他自己去团队里求角色），不是回答只要知道目标就能做什么，属张冠李戴式的\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：to continue to pursue roles within teams that I knew could teach and challenge me\nD) 与原文相反\nFollow their own path. 借用第四段 my path 的说法，但原文是作者说我不认为自己这条\n路是唯一的，恰恰是不主张人人照走他的路，选项却说去走自己的路，与原文倾向相反。\n借用原文：That's not to say that I think my path is the only one.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Why did the school principal ask what needed to change?", "options": ["A) To respond actively to students' requests.", "B) To engage students more in their learning.", "C) To enable teachers to interact more with their students.", "D) To allow students more freedom in their academic work."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To engage students more in their learning.\n【定位】When planning for this year, our principal asked what needed to change to engage students\nmore in their learning.\n在筹划今年的工作时，我们校长问需要改变什么，好让学生更投入到自己的学习中。\n【信号】目的状语 to engage… 直接给出动机。句中 to engage students more in their learning\n是\"为了…\"的目的状语，紧跟在校长发问之后，正是他发问的目的所在，是这句成为答案句\n的信号。\n【替换】to engage students more in their learning ↔ To engage students more in their learning.\n几乎原句搬到选项\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项说\"主动回应学生的请求\"，但录音里是校长主动发问、要让学生更投入，没有\"回应学生\n请求\"一说，方向不符。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项把焦点说成\"让老师与学生更多互动\"，盗用了录音反复出现的 teachers、students，但\n校长的目的是让\"学生\"更投入学习，不是促进师生互动，偷换了主体与重点。\n借用原文：to engage students more in their learning\nD) 过度推断\n选项说\"给学生更多学业自由\"，录音目的句只讲 engage（投入），从未提 freedom（自\n由），是把投入度过度引申成自由度。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What has flexible seating meant at the speaker's school?", "options": ["A) Using new furniture to create a comfortable environment.", "B) Shifting from traditional teaching to task-based learning.", "C) Adopting a variety of different seating options.", "D) Rearranging most traditional chairs and desks."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Adopting a variety of different seating options.\n【定位】For us, flexible seating has meant removing most of the traditional chairs and desks and\nintroducing a variety of different seating options to promote student engagement.\n对我们来说，灵活座位意味着撤掉大部分传统桌椅，引入多种不同的座位选择，以促进学生\n投入。\n【信号】下定义信号 has meant。句中 flexible seating has meant 是\"在本校它意味着什么\"的下\n定义信号，与题干 has meant 完全对应，定义内容紧随其后，是答案句的标志。\n【替换】introducing a variety of different seating options ↔\nAdopting a variety of different seating options.\nintroducing 与 Adopting 同义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项说\"用新家具营造舒适环境\"，录音定义句讲的是 promote student engagement（促\n进投入），从未提 comfortable（舒适），属凭空添加。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项说\"从传统教学转向任务式学习\"，录音只说从 teacher-focused 转向 student-\nfocused，并未出现 task-based learning（任务式学习）这一说法，偷换了概念。\n借用原文：a shift in practice from being teacher-focused to student-focused learning\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项\"重排大部分传统桌椅\"盗用了录音的 traditional chairs and desks，但录音是\nremoving（撤掉）这些桌椅再引入多样座位，不是 Rearranging（重排），只截了动作的\n一半且改了动词。\n借用原文：removing most of the traditional chairs and desks", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What has flexible seating brought about at the speaker's school?", "options": ["A) A win for all people involved.", "B) A strengthened effect of its discipline.", "C) A harmonious relationship among its students.", "D) A change to teachers' opinion of their students."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A win for all people involved.\n【定位】From our experience so far, flexible seating enhances student ownership of space and\nengagement in learning, while reducing rates of student disengagement and disciplinary\nactions. It is a win for all concerned.\n从我们目前的经验看，灵活座位增强了学生对空间的归属感和学习投入，同时降低了学生的\n脱离感和纪律处分的发生率。这对所有相关方都是一种共赢。\n【信号】结尾总结句 It is a win for all concerned。末段 It is a win for all concerned 是带总结\n语气的收束句，把前面列举的好处一句话归纳为\"对所有人都是共赢\"，是结果题最该锁的\n落点。\n【替换】It is a win for all concerned ↔ A win for all people involved.\nall concerned 与 all people involved 同义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项说\"纪律效果增强\"，盗用录音的 disciplinary，但录音是 reducing…disciplinary\nactions（减少纪律处分），方向相反；且只取纪律一项，丢了\"共赢\"的全面性。\n借用原文：reducing rates of student disengagement and disciplinary actions\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项说\"学生之间关系融洽\"，盗用了高频词 students，但录音讲的是学生对空间的归属感与\n投入，从未提学生彼此之间的 harmonious relationship（融洽关系）。\n借用原文：enhances student ownership of space and engagement in learning\nD) 未提及\n选项说\"改变了老师对学生的看法\"，录音末段只讲对学生投入、纪律的成效与\"共赢\"，并未\n提到老师对学生看法的任何改变。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Why were dozens of British students sent home on Tuesday?", "options": ["A) They weren't informed of the school's updated rules.", "B) They arrived late for their first day of school.", "C) They answered their headteacher's email in an impolite way.", "D) They didn't wear the shoes required by the school's new policy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They didn't wear the shoes required by the school's new policy.\n【定位】About 30 students were turned away from Taverham High School in Norfolk, England.\nHeadteacher, Dr Roger Harris, confirmed that this was due to a change in the school's\nuniform policy.\n大约 30 名学生被英格兰诺福克的 Taverham 高中拒之门外。校长 Roger Harris 博士确\n认，这是由于学校校服政策的一项变动。\n【信号】因果信号 due to。答案句里 this was due to a change in the school's uniform policy\n的 \"due to\" 是明确的因果信号，它后面跟的\"校服政策变动\"就是被送回家的原因，正是\nWhy 题要抓的落点。\n【替换】a change in the school's uniform policy ↔ the shoes required by the school's new policy\n概括换具体加同义改写\n【排除】A)\n选项说学生\"没被告知更新的规定\"，但录音里校长明确说 he notified parents of the\nupdated rules in an email in June——是已经通知过的，选项把\"通知过\"反说成\"没被告\n知\"，方向相反。\n借用原文：he notified parents of the updated rules in an email in June\nB)\n录音只说这天是 their first day of school on Tuesday，提到了\"开学第一天\"，但从未说学\n生\"迟到\"；选项盗用了\"first day\"的时间背景，把原因编成\"迟到\"，原文没有依据。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音提到校长是用 in an email in June 通知家长的，选项盗用\"email\"这个原词，编出\"以\n不礼貌方式回复校长邮件\"；原文只说校长发邮件通知，根本没说学生回邮件、更没说态度\n不礼貌。\n借用原文：he notified parents of the updated rules in an email in June", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What can the price of school uniforms spark among families in financial difficulty?", "options": ["A) Riots.", "B) Anger.", "C) Disputes.", "D) Panic."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Panic.\n【定位】The price of school uniforms can spark panic among families struggling with the high cost\nof living.\n校服的价格会在那些在高生活成本中苦苦挣扎的家庭里激起恐慌。\n【信号】题干原词 spark 几乎原句重现。答案句 The price of school uniforms can spark panic\namong families 与题干用词高度重合（price/spark/families），spark 紧跟的那个词\npanic 就是被引发的反应，是定位信号。\n【替换】spark panic among families ↔ Panic.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A)\nRiots（骚乱）程度最重、最抓耳，但录音里从未出现\"骚乱\"；它是把\"恐慌\"夸大成\"骚乱\"的\n诱饵，原文无依据。\nB)\nAnger（愤怒）听上去和\"家长有意见\"很搭，但原文 spark 后面接的是 panic 不是 anger；\nanger 是个貌似合理却没被念到的情绪词。\nC)\nDisputes（争执/纠纷）也是一种貌似合理的负面反应，但原文激起的是 panic，并未提\n到\"争执\"，属没被念到的干扰项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What did the headteacher think of the school's new uniform rules?", "options": ["A) They strengthened the school's discipline and order.", "B) They improved student behavior and performance.", "C) They enhanced the positive image of the school.", "D) They rendered the school unique in the district."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They improved student behavior and performance.\n【定位】Harris held that strengthened rules around school uniforms improved student outcomes\nand behavior.\nHarris 认为，更严格的校服规定改善了学生的成绩和行为。\n【信号】观点引导词 held that。答案句 Harris held that strengthened rules around school\nuniforms improved student outcomes and behavior 里的 \"held that\"（认为）是明确\n的观点信号，引出的正是校长本人的看法，是态度题的落点。\n【替换】improved student outcomes and behavior ↔ improved student behavior and performance\n同义词替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里有 strengthened rules around school uniforms，\"strengthened\" 这个词真的念\n到了；选项 strengthened the school's discipline and order 盗用 strengthened，但把\n它的对象换成\"纪律和秩序\"——原文是\"更严格的规定改善了成绩和行为\"，strengthened\n修饰的是 rules 而非 discipline，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：strengthened rules around school uniforms improved student outcomes and behavior\nC)\n\"提升学校的正面形象\"（positive image）听起来是个合理好处，但校长说的是改善\"成绩和\n行为\"，从未提到\"形象\"，原文无依据。\nD)\n录音第 4 段出现过 Private rental prices in Britain 等带 district/地区背景的经济描述（原\n文用 households/Britain），选项 rendered the school unique in the district 借\"地\n区\"这类背景感造出\"让学校在本区独一无二\"，但校长的观点里根本没说学校\"独特\"，属编\n造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What did Lucinda May have to do to buy her child the correct pair of shoes?", "options": ["A) Turn to her parents for help.", "B) Tighten her monthly budget.", "C) Borrow £65 from her friend.", "D) Postpone paying her other bills."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Turn to her parents for help.\n【定位】Lucinda May, mum of a Taverham student, said that she had to ask her parents for £65 to buy\nher child the correct pair of shoes.\nTaverham 一名学生的母亲 Lucinda May 说，她不得不向自己的父母要 65 英镑，来给孩子\n买那双符合要求的鞋。\n【信号】被问者的直接动作 had to ask。答案句 she had to ask her parents for £65 to buy her\nchild the correct pair of shoes 里的 \"had to ask\" 正是题干问的\"不得不做的动作\"，her\nparents 点明动作对象，是定位信号。\n【替换】ask her parents for £65 ↔ Turn to her parents for help\n同义短语 + 概括化\n【排除】B)\n\"缩紧月度预算\"和第 4 段 many households are on tight budgets 的 \"tight budgets\" 背\n景相呼应，但那是说大环境下家庭预算紧，并非 May 本人\"缩预算\"这个动作；她的实际做\n法是向父母要钱。\n借用原文：many households are on tight budgets\nC) 偷换概念\n金额 £65 是原文真念到的（ask her parents for £65），选项 Borrow £65 from her\nfriend. 保住了对的金额却把对象从\"her parents\"偷换成\"her friend\"，动作也\n从\"ask\"变\"borrow\"，对象错了。\n借用原文：she had to ask her parents for £65\nD)\n\"推迟支付其他账单\"和第 4 段 Soaring energy bills have made things worse. 里的 bills\n沾边，但原文只说能源账单让情况更糟，从没说 May\"推迟付账单\"；这是借 bills 一词编出\n的动作。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) chance\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 their 物主代词后 + of 短语，名词性短语中心词\n【依据】chance of doing sth = 做……的可能性\n原句意为「他们相信这样能最大化做出最佳决定的可能性」，maximize the chance of\ndoing sth 是非常稳定的搭配，C 完美契合。\n【竞争词】B) case case of doing 不是「可能性」\ncase 表示「案例、情况」，maximize their case of making 既不成搭配，也与「最大化可能\n性」的语义不符。\nN) selection selection 表「选择动作/选出物」，与 maximize 不搭\nselection of making the best decision 语义混乱（做最佳决定本身不是「被选出物」），且 N\n在 27 题处与 making a ___ 搭配更稳，应留给 27 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) selection\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 后 + from 短语前，名词作宾语\n【依据】make a selection from = 从……中做出选择\nmake a selection 是英语里表「做出一次选择」的高频搭配，from all of these choices 表\n「从所有这些选项中」，正好契合 when it comes to actually making a __(27)__ from all\nof these choices 想表达的「真要在这些选项里挑一个」。\n【竞争词】B) case make a case 义为「论证、辩护」，方向跑偏\nmake a case for sth 表「为某事辩护、提出论据」，与「从一堆选项里挑一个」的语义完全不\n沾边。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) confused\n形容词\n【词性槽】become 后接形容词/过去分词作表语\n【依据】confused = 困惑、不知所措，正好引出「干脆不决定」\npeople can become __(28)__ and avoid making a decision altogether，「变得困惑 →\n索性不做决定」是顺承的因果，confused 描述心理状态贴合上文 when it comes to\nactually making a __(27)__ from all of these choices 的为难感。\n【竞争词】E) crucial 义为「重要的」，与「不做决定」矛盾\n若代入「become crucial」=「变得重要」，与并列动作「干脆不做决定」逻辑相反，明显方向\n错位；且 E 更适合后面 31 题描述「决定本身变得重要」。\nL) regular 义为「定期的、常规的」，与心理状态不沾边\nregular 描述事物的频率/规律，「become regular」（变得规律）与本句心理负担的语境毫无\n关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) invest\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 people 后缺谓语，且与 feel less confident 并列\n【依据】invest more in sth = 在……上投入更多\nthey __(29)__ more in the decision 想说「在这个决定上投入更多（精力/心思）」，\ninvest more in sth 是固定搭配，与下文 but feel less confident 形成「投入更多 → 反而\n更没信心」的转折，是文章解释「选择悖论」的关键句。\n【竞争词】G) enter enter in 不成搭配\nenter 通常直接接宾语（enter a room）或接 into，没有 enter more in the decision 的说法；\n语义上也无法表达「投入精力」。\nI) matter matter 是不及物动词「要紧」，不能接 more in\nmatter 表「要紧」时句式是「sth matters」，不可说「they matter more in the\ndecision」；且 I 的「要紧」义应留给 32 题。\nM) seize seize 是「抓住」，常与机会搭配，与「投入更多」不符\nseize the chance/opportunity 是常用搭配，seize more in the decision 既不成搭配也不合\n语义。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— F deposited 与 H invest 都跟「投入」沾边，最迷惑。\nthey __(29)__ more in the decision，考生看到 deposited 也有「存放、投入」之意会犹\n豫；防坑：先看形态——will/they 现在时后只能接动词原形，deposited 直接淘汰；再看\n搭配——invest in 才是正解。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) presented\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 are 后 + 介词 with，典型「be + 形容词 + with」结构\n【依据】be presented with 是固定搭配，表「被给予/面对……」\nwhen we are __(30)__ with more choices 要表达「当我们被给予/面对更多选择时」，be\npresented with sth 正是英语里表「被呈上/面对某物」的标准搭配，与下文 making the\n\"right\" or \"correct\" decision begins to feel more __(31)__ 形成「面对多选 → 决定显得\n更重要」的因果链。\n【竞争词】D) confused confused with 表「把……与……搞混」，方向反了，且已被 28 题用走\nconfused 形容人 confused by/about 才地道，confused with 多用于「把 A 和 B 搞混」\n（confuse A with B），与本句「面对更多选择」语义不符；并且按解题顺序 D 已先被 28 题\npeople can become __(28)__ 用走，不可再选。\nE) crucial 义为「至关重要的」，与 with 不搭\ncrucial 后通常接 to/for/in，没有 be crucial with 的用法；意思也偏离「面对更多选择」的\n语境。\nL) regular 义为「定期的、常规的」，与上下文无关\nregular 与「面对更多选择」毫无语义勾连，be regular with 也不构成本句所需的「面对/被给\n予」之意。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) crucial\n形容词\n【词性槽】feel + more + 空，feel 后接形容词作表语\n【依据】和 difficult 并列：决定既「显得更重要」又「更难做」\nfeel more __(31)__ and, at the same time, more difficult to do 用 and 连接两个并列形\n容词，crucial（重要的）与 difficult（困难的）一起描绘「正确决定变得既重要又难做」，\n正好引出下文 the deep fear that we will make the wrong decision 的「深深的恐惧」。\n【竞争词】L) regular 义为「常规的」，与「深深的恐惧」逻辑不通\n若说「正确决定显得更常规、更难做」既自相矛盾（常规的事不该难），也无法解释下文为何会\n引出「害怕选错」的恐惧。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) matter\n动词\n【词性槽】助动词 not 后 + 副词 much，需要动词原形\n【依据】matter much = 关系重大、要紧\nmany of the choices you make, such as what to have for lunch, will not __(32)__\nmuch in the future 想说「你做的很多选择，比如午餐吃什么，将来不会有什么要紧的」，\nwill not matter much 是常见表达，与作者主张「把选择放进合适视角」的对策正相呼应。\n【竞争词】M) seize seize 是及物动词，与不及物结构不符\nseize 后通常接具体宾语（机会、权力），will not seize much in the future 既缺宾语也不合\n「不重要」之意。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) ultimately\n副词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后 + 被动语态 be changed 前，副词作状语\n【依据】ultimately 表「最终归根到底」，弱化决定的不可逆感\neven more important choices, like accepting a new job, can __(33)__ be changed 想\n说「就算是接受新工作这种更重要的选择，归根到底也是可以变的」，与上句 will not\n__(32)__ much in the future 共同构成「把选择放进合适视角」的两条理由。\n【竞争词】A) approximately approximately 表「大约」，修饰数字而非动作\napproximately 用来修饰数量（approximately 100 people），用在 can approximately be\nchanged 既语义不通，也与「最终也可以改」的安抚作用不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) enter\n动词\n【词性槽】并列 to + 空，需动词原形；与上句 to remember 并列\n【依据】enter these situations with ... = 带着……进入这些情境\nto __(34)__ these situations with clear guidelines and ideas of what you want，「带着\n清晰的指引和想法去进入/着手这些情境」，enter a situation with sth 是地道说法，表\n「以某种状态/准备去面对一个局面」，与下文 which can narrow the possible choices 形\n成「带着准备进入 → 缩小可能选项」的因果链。\n【竞争词】M) seize seize 是「抓住、夺取」，对象多为机会/权力\nseize these situations 表「抓住/夺取这些情境」语义生硬，且与 with clear guidelines 这\n种「带着方法论」的伴随状语搭不上——seize 强调瞬间、强力的占有，不是「带着准备去\n面对」。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— seize these situations 单看也通，最难分辨。to __(34)__\nthese situations with clear guidelines，「抓住这些情境」看似有力，但 seize 强调强力\n夺取、对象多为机会；防坑：联读伴随状语 with clear guidelines and ideas，作者要的是\n「带着方法论从容进入」，enter 才是对的动作气质。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) approximately", "B) case", "C) chance", "D) confused", "E) crucial", "F) deposited", "G) enter", "H) invest", "I) matter", "J) presented", "K) range", "L) regular", "M) seize", "N) selection", "O) ultimately"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) range\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后 + of 短语，典型「冠词+名词+of...」结构\n【依据】a range of / the range of options 是固定搭配，意为「一系列选项」\n原句 from the __(35)__ of options 想表达「从这一系列选项中挑出你想要的」，range of\noptions 是英语里非常稳定的搭配，正好对应。这也呼应了第一段 as many choices as\npossible 所说的「众多选项」。\n【竞争词】B) case case of options 不成搭配\ncase 常见的是 in case of、a case of（一箱/一例），不能用 the case of options 表示「一系\n列选项」，语义和搭配都不通。\nC) chance 意为「机会」，与「一组选项」错位，且已被 26 题用走\nchance 意为机会、可能性，the chance of options 既不是固定搭配，也不符合「从众多选项里\n选」的语义；而且按解题顺序它早已在 26 题被先用掉。\nN) selection 义近但要留给 27 题的「做出一次选择」\nselection 也能表「一组选择」，但本篇 27 题 making a __(27)__ from all of these choices 与\nmake a selection（做出选择）搭配更紧、更不可替代，应优先把 N 留给 27 题；35 题用\nrange 反而最稳。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "An increasing amount of data indicates that installing bike lanes raises local business revenues.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】increasing amount of data + raises local business revenues\n【定位】There's a rising amount of data showing that installing bike lanes and making streets more\npedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes of a place.\n越来越多的数据表明,铺设自行车道与让街道更适合步行,能拉升一个地方的经济运势。\n【改写】An increasing amount of data indicates ↔ There's a rising amount of data showing\nincreasing↔rising、indicates↔showing 同义替换\ninstalling bike lanes raises local business revenues ↔\ninstalling bike lanes and making streets more pedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes\nof a place\nraises↔boosts 同义、local business revenues↔economic fortunes 上位概括\n【辨邻】 G a survey of research from 23 cities 是一次性的综述结果,而题干 An increasing\namount of data 强调的是数据在不断增多的'势',语义重心不同。\na survey of research from 23 cities found that bike lanes and pedestrian-friendly design didn't hurt\nlocal retail and food stores\nF In 2013, researchers at New York City's Department of Transportation studied\nseven stretches of road 是单次、具体的研究,不承担'数据日益增多'这一总括语义。\nby the third year, sales grew faster on five of the streets than in the district overall", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to a magazine writer, some small business owners' resistance to bike lanes arises from cultural factors.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】a magazine writer + cultural factors\n【定位】For others, the pushback is cultural, says Henry Grabar, a writer for the Slate Magazine.\nSlate 杂志撰稿人 Henry Grabar 表示,对另一些商户来说,这种抵触是文化层面的。\n【改写】According to a magazine writer ↔ says Henry Grabar, a writer for the Slate Magazine\n具体人名→类指身份的概括\nsome small business owners' resistance to bike lanes arises from cultural factors ↔\nFor others, the pushback is cultural\n形容词→名词化、resistance↔pushback 同义\n【辨邻】 J she sees the parking loss as an existential risk 强调的是经济上的生存威胁,与'文\n化'无关。\nFor our business, the bike lanes are way worse than Covid was\nL Psychology beats all 与 culture wars over climate change 虽都和'文化'相关,但没有\n引用任何具名杂志撰稿人,不满足 According to a magazine writer 的来源限制。\nThe fierce divide between store owners and bike-lane advocates seems similar to our larger culture\nwars over climate change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A stretch of Skillman Ave witnessed not only a big increase in sales but also the opening of new businesses.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Skillman Ave + 销售大增 + 新店开张\n【定位】In the year after the bike lanes arrived, businesses on Skillman saw sales rise by 12 percent,\ncompared to 3 percent for Queens in general. What's more, that section of road saw new\nbusinesses open, while Queens overall had a net loss.\n自行车道铺设后的一年里,Skillman 沿街商户的销售上涨了 12%,相比之下整个皇后区只涨了\n3%;不仅如此,这一段路还有新店开张,而皇后区整体出现净减少。\n【改写】A stretch of Skillman Ave witnessed not only a big increase in sales ↔\nbusinesses on Skillman saw sales rise by 12 percent\n动词名词化 + 具体数字→概括程度\nbut also the opening of new businesses ↔ that section of road saw new businesses open\n动词 open 名词化为 the opening\n【辨邻】 A would ruin stores and restaurants along Skillman 是事前的'担忧将来时',与题干 a\nbig increase in sales 的事后正面结果方向相反。\nTaking out those parking spots, as they argued at protests and in letters to the city council, would\nruin stores and restaurants along Skillman\nB asked the city's Department of Finance to give him a few years' worth of sales\nfigures for that stretch of Skillman Ave 只是记者去要数据的过程,不承载具体增长数\n据与新店信息。\nHow had the businesses on that street fared?\nD Many still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city 是商户的主观坚持,\n不是题干要求的'销售大增 + 新店开张'两件并列事实。\nonly a few store owners admitted the lanes had helped", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "When people have no way out in crises, they are more likely to embrace changes.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】no way out in crises + embrace changes\n【定位】Crises, after all, have a way of opening people's eyes to possibilities.\n危机毕竟有它的办法,能让人睁开眼睛看到新的可能性。\n【改写】When people have no way out in crises ↔\nDuring Covid, restaurants and cafés lost so much business that cities nationwide began\nallowing them to build curbside seating areas\n危机举例 + no way out 来自下文支撑\nthey are more likely to embrace changes ↔\nCrises, after all, have a way of opening people's eyes to possibilities\nembrace changes↔opening eyes to possibilities 同义\n【辨邻】 N until enough of the public is finally concerned about climate change 的触发条件\n是'公众担忧气候变化',与题干 in crises 不同。\nMaybe bike lanes will always be charged with emotion, until enough of the public is finally\nconcerned about climate change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "According to one survey, city design with bike lanes and pedestrian space did not negatively impact local businesses.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】one survey + did not negatively impact\n【定位】Beyond New York, a survey of research from 23 cities found that bike lanes and pedestrian-\nfriendly design didn't hurt local retail and food stores.\n把视野放到纽约以外,一项汇总了 23 个城市研究的调查发现,自行车道与步行友好设计并未损\n害本地零售与餐饮店。\n【改写】According to one survey ↔ a survey of research from 23 cities\none survey↔a survey 同义\ncity design with bike lanes and pedestrian space did not negatively impact local businesses ↔\nbike lanes and pedestrian-friendly design didn't hurt local retail and food stores\ndidn't hurt↔did not negatively impact、retail and food↔local businesses 上位\n【辨邻】 F by the third year, sales grew faster on five of the streets than in the district\noverall 是 study 的正向增长结论,与题干 did not negatively impact 的否定式中性结\n论语气不一致。\nresearchers at New York City's Department of Transportation studied seven stretches of road\nE There's a rising amount of data showing that installing bike lanes ... boosts the\neconomic fortunes of a place 是趋势性表述,不是 one survey 所指的单次研究。\nThere's a rising amount of data showing that installing bike lanes and making streets more\npedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes of a place", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Despite increased business activity on Skillman Ave, many store owners insisted the bike lanes were destroying their area.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Skillman Ave + 商业其实增长 + 店主仍坚称车道毁了街区\n【定位】When Coburn spoke to them and described what he'd found, only a few store owners\nadmitted the lanes had helped. Many still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city.\n当 Coburn 找店主、把自己的发现告诉他们时,只有少数店主承认车道帮了忙;许多人仍坚称\n车道正在毁掉他们所在的这一带。\n【改写】Despite increased business activity on Skillman Ave ↔\nonly a few store owners admitted the lanes had helped\n让步从句概括前文事实\nmany store owners insisted the bike lanes were destroying their area ↔\nMany still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city\ninsisted 原样、killing↔destroying 同义、area↔part of the city 同义\n【辨邻】 A would ruin stores and restaurants along Skillman 是 Five years ago 车道还没铺时\n的反对,A 段里没有 Despite increased business activity 这种'尽管商业活动增长'的事\n实背景。\nTaking out those parking spots, as they argued at protests and in letters to the city council, would\nruin stores and restaurants along Skillman\nC businesses on Skillman saw sales rise by 12 percent 只讲数据,没讲店主的坚持态\n度,题干信息重心在 D。\nbusinesses on Skillman saw sales rise by 12 percent", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "In a war of culture, data does not help much in changing people's minds.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】war of culture + data does not help\n【定位】If we've learned anything about culture wars, it's that data isn't much good at changing minds.\n如果说我们在文化战上学到了什么,那就是: 数据并不擅长改变人的想法。\n【改写】In a war of culture ↔ our larger culture wars over climate change\nculture wars↔a war of culture 名词组顺序倒换\ndata does not help much in changing people's minds ↔ data isn't much good at changing minds\nisn't much good at↔does not help much in 同义否定结构\n【辨邻】 O If data won't change minds, customers might 的论述重心是'顾客'这条出路,且整段\n没有提到 culture war,不满足题干第一枚指纹。\nIf data won't change minds, customers might", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A businesswoman said her business dropped sharply when parking space gave way to a bike lane, because most of her customers came by car.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一位女商户 + 生意骤降 + 顾客多开车\n【定位】She said business fell by at least 40 percent when the city removed nearby parking to put in a\nbike lane. The majority of her customers drive, with many coming from nearby towns.\n她说,当城市拆掉附近的停车位以铺设自行车道时,生意至少下降了 40%;她的大多数顾客都是\n开车来的,很多来自邻近城镇。\n【改写】A businesswoman said her business dropped sharply when parking space gave way to a bike lane ↔\nShe said business fell by at least 40 percent when the city removed nearby parking to put in a\nbike lane\nhairdresser↔businesswoman、fell 40%↔dropped sharply、removed parking to put in a\nbike lane↔parking space gave way to a bike lane\nbecause most of her customers came by car ↔ The majority of her customers drive\nthe majority↔most、drive↔came by car 同义\n【辨邻】 A Parking here is already a nightmare, one shouted at a protest rally 是匿名群体抗\n议口号,不是题干 A businesswoman said 的具体个体讲述。\nremoving 116 parking spots", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Local business owners on Skillman Ave argued that their businesses would be ruined when parking spots were replaced by bike lanes.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Skillman Ave + 店主担心被毁 + 停车位被换成自行车道\n【定位】Cyclists loved the plan, but local business owners got very angry. Taking out those parking\nspots, as they argued at protests and in letters to the city council, would ruin stores and\nrestaurants along Skillman.\n骑行者很喜欢这个方案,但当地商户非常愤怒;他们在抗议中、在写给市议会的信里都辩称,拆\n掉那些停车位会毁掉 Skillman 沿街的商店与餐馆。\n【改写】Local business owners on Skillman Ave argued that their businesses would be ruined ↔\nlocal business owners got very angry. Taking out those parking spots, as they argued at\nprotests and in letters to the city council, would ruin stores and restaurants along Skillman\nlocal business owners 原样、argued 原样、ruin↔be ruined 主被动换\nwhen parking spots were replaced by bike lanes ↔\nputting bike lanes onto a stretch of Skillman Ave — and removing 116 parking spots\nputting in + removing↔replaced by 概括同义\n【辨邻】 D Many still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city 表达事后的现在/过\n去进行时态,而题干 would be ruined 是事前将来时态。\nMany still insisted the lanes were killing their part of the city\nJ Cindy Hughes, a hairdresser(理发师) in Cambridge, Massachusetts 明确发生地在\nCambridge,且 business fell by at least 40 percent 是事后实测,与题干 Skillman\nAve 与事前担忧均不符。\nbusiness fell by at least 40 percent when the city removed nearby parking to put in a bike lane", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "People may find it more pleasant to wander around streets redesigned for cycling and walking.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】more pleasant + wander/stroll + redesigned streets\n【定位】streets redesigned for bikes and pedestrians tend to become more pleasant places to\nhang around\n为自行车和行人重新设计过的街道,往往变成让人更愿意逗留的宜人场所。\n【改写】People may find it more pleasant to wander around streets redesigned for cycling and walking ↔\nstreets redesigned for bikes and pedestrians tend to become more pleasant places to hang\naround\nredesigned for bikes and pedestrians↔redesigned for cycling and walking、hang\naround↔wander around、more pleasant 原样\n【辨邻】 E making streets more pedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes of a\nplace 重心在经济提升,不涉及题干 more pleasant to wander around 的体验感受。\ninstalling bike lanes and making streets more pedestrian-friendly boosts the economic fortunes of\na place\nG bike lanes and pedestrian-friendly design didn't hurt local retail and food\nstores 讨论的是商业影响,不是题干所要的'更宜人逛街'体验。\nFears of disastrous consequences for local businesses are unfounded", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the timing of our behaviors?", "options": ["A) It has a considerable impact on our health.", "B) It confines us to a 24-hour day-night cycle.", "C) It requires us to follow a particular rhythm.", "D) It holds the key to all human body functions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about ... 问的是文章就「行为时机」明说了什么，\n答案应能在原文直接找到对应句，不需推断。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn from the passage about the timing of our behaviors? 是一个\nwhat 引导的特殊疑问句，主干是「我们从文中得知什么」，about 短语把范围锁定在「我\n们行为的时机」上。结构平铺直叙，无从句、无否定，唯一要抓的是 about 后面的话题\n词，它指明这道题只问「行为时机」相关的内容。\n【定位】Consequently, all human body functions also follow this daily rhythm, and the timing of\nbehaviors like exercise or food intake can significantly influence your health.\n因此，人体的所有功能也都遵循这一日常节律，而锻炼、进食等行为的时机会显著影响你的\n健康。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncan significantly influence your health ↔ It has a considerable impact on our health\n预判「行为时机大大影响健康」与 It has a considerable impact on our health 完全吻\n合，故锁定该项。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是「昼夜周期导致行为有节律」，It confines us to a 24-hour day-night cycle 却\n说「行为时机把我们限制在 24 小时周期里」，把因当成了果，与原文 This cycle is the\nreason why people rest at night and are active during the day 相悖。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt requires us to follow a particular rhythm 说「行为时机要求我们遵循某节律」，但\n原文里「遵循节律」的主体是人体功能（all human body functions also follow this\ndaily rhythm），并非由「行为时机」提出要求。\nD) 过度推断\nIt holds the key to all human body functions 称行为时机「掌握着人体所有功能的关\n键」，原文只说人体功能遵循节律、行为时机影响健康，从未说时机决定一切功能，all\n与 holds the key 属夸大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say about the proteins in our body?", "options": ["A) They can modify the effects of medicines in different ways.", "B) They can reduce unwanted side effects of certain medicines.", "C) Their reaction to medicines changes during the day-night cycle.", "D) Their design determines how differently they react to medicines."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the proteins 问作者对蛋白质的明确陈述，答案落在讲\n蛋白质的那句话里，属客观查找而非推断。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about the proteins in our body? 主干是「作者关于我\n们体内的蛋白质说了什么」，say about 后面的 the proteins in our body 是话题。注意四\n个选项都是完整句子，需要逐个判断哪句和原文一致，题干本身无陷阱。\n【定位】This means that the specific proteins a drug is designed to modify can react differently to the\nmedicine over the course of a 24-hour time period.\n这意味着，药物所要作用的那些特定蛋白质，在 24 小时的进程中对药物的反应可能各不\n相同。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey can modify the effects of medicines in different ways 说「蛋白质改变药物的\n效果」，但原文是药物去作用（modify）蛋白质（the specific proteins a drug is\ndesigned to modify），施受关系正好相反。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey can reduce unwanted side effects of certain medicines 把「减少副作用」说\n成蛋白质的能力，可原文里减副作用的是「按特定时间服药」这一做法（taking\nmedicines at specific times could help increase their effectiveness and reduce\nunwanted side effects），与蛋白质无关。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncan react differently to the medicine over the course of a 24-hour time period ↔\nTheir reaction to medicines changes during the day-night cycle\n预判「蛋白质对药反应随时段变化」正是 Their reaction to medicines changes\nduring the day-night cycle 所述，锁定该项。\nD) 无中生有\nTheir design determines how differently they react to medicines 称「蛋白质的\n设计决定其反应差异」，原文从未谈蛋白质有何 design，更没说设计决定反应，纯属\n臆造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do doctors do when prescribing medicine for people?", "options": ["A) They give little thought to the time of taking it for maximum effect.", "B) They rarely consider which medicine works better for which patient.", "C) They tell patients its possible side effects during a period of 24 hours.", "D) They tell patients to comply with the directions of drug manufacturers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do doctors do when prescribing medicine 问医生实际做了什么，是可在原文直接\n核对的事实。\n【拆句】题干 What do doctors do when prescribing medicine for people? 主干「医生开药时做\n什么」，when prescribing 是时间状语（现在分词作状语，相当于 when they\nprescribe）。结构清晰，关键是抓 doctors 与 prescribing 这一动作场景。\n【定位】When doctors prescribe medicine for people, they rarely consider the best time to take it.\n医生给人们开药时，很少考虑服药的最佳时间。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthey rarely consider the best time to take it ↔\nThey give little thought to the time of taking it for maximum effect\n预判「医生很少考虑最佳服药时间」与 They give little thought to the time of taking\nit for maximum effect 一致，锁定该项。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey rarely consider which medicine works better for which patient 把疏忽的对象\n换成「哪种药对哪个病人更好」，而原文疏忽的是服药「时间」，话题被悄悄换掉。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey tell patients its possible side effects during a period of 24 hours 说医生告知\n24 小时内的副作用，原文讲的是药物未被研究其昼夜差异（most drugs have not\nbeen studied for possible different effects during a 24-hour cycle），并非医生告\n知副作用。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey tell patients to comply with the directions of drug manufacturers 说让病人遵\n从药厂说明，但原文 compliance 指病人遵从医嘱按时服药（patients are directed to\ntake most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to ensure\ncompliance），并未提 drug manufacturers。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why do doctors advise patients to take most drugs in the morning or in the evening?", "options": ["A) To discourage them from making complaints.", "B) To ensure they take the drugs as instructed.", "C) To comply with new research findings strictly.", "D) To guarantee the maximum effect of the drugs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why do doctors advise ... 问的是原文已经给出的理由，定位到目的状语即可作答，不需自\n己推因果。\n【拆句】题干 Why do doctors advise patients to take most drugs in the morning or in the\nevening? 是 why 引导的因果问句，主干「医生为何建议病人在早上或晚上服大多数药」。\nadvise sb. to do 是「建议某人做某事」结构。问 why 意味着答案是原文里这一建议背后的\n目的或原因。\n【定位】Therefore, patients are directed to take most drugs during the morning or evening primarily to\nensure compliance.\n因此，病人被指示在早上或晚上服用大多数药物，主要是为了确保遵医服药。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTo discourage them from making complaints 说目的是「阻止病人投诉」，原文目的\n只有 ensure compliance，从未提 complaints，凭空捏造。\nB) ✓ 正确\nto ensure compliance ↔ To ensure they take the drugs as instructed\n预判「为确保病人按医嘱服药」与 To ensure they take the drugs as instructed 同\n义，锁定该项。\nC) 与原文相反\nTo comply with new research findings strictly 说目的是严格遵循新研究成果，但原文\n恰恰指出多数药未做昼夜研究（most drugs have not been studied），医生让早晚服\n药并非依据研究，方向相反。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo guarantee the maximum effect of the drugs 说目的是保证药效最大，但全文论点\n正是医生没考虑增效时机（they rarely consider the best time to take it），早晚服药\n并非为了药效最大化，正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author suggest near the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Considering drug-taking timing when prescribing drugs for patients.", "B) Making treatments less complex by taking drug timing into account.", "C) Conducting more studies to find out the best timing for treating different diseases.", "D) Finding out the most effective drugs for treating diseases through further research."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest near the end 问作者在收尾处的整体主张，需要把末段的\n呼吁概括出来，而非抓某个孤立细节。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author suggest near the end of the passage? 主干「作者在文章接\n近结尾处建议了什么」，suggest 在此是「建议、主张」，near the end of the passage\n是地点状语，明确把答案锁在文章末段。这是一道带位置提示的建议题。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nConsidering drug-taking timing when prescribing drugs for patients 是第三段对\n医生开药的观点延伸，而题干问的是 near the end 的建议；末段真正的呼吁是「做更多\n研究」，而非这条局部主张。\nB) 无中生有\nMaking treatments less complex by taking drug timing into account 称考虑服\n药时机可让治疗「不那么复杂」，原文末段只说能让治疗更有效、惠及更多人（could\nhelp make treatments more effective and help more people worldwide），从未提\nless complex。\nC) ✓ 正确\nMore research is needed to determine the best times to administer treatments for different\ndiseases\n↔ Conducting more studies to find out the best timing for treating different diseases\n预判「需更多研究找最佳给药时机」与 Conducting more studies to find out the\nbest timing for treating different diseases 完全对应，锁定该项。\nD) 偷换概念\nFinding out the most effective drugs for treating diseases through further\nresearch 说要研究「最有效的药物」，可末段研究的目标是「最佳给药时间」（the\nbest times to administer treatments），全文谈的是时机而非药物本身，对象被\n偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the economics professor and her hairdresser?", "options": ["A) They differ in their reasons for continuing to work.", "B) They are both committed to working into their 70s.", "C) They are happy about their current financial situation.", "D) They hold different views about postponing retirement."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the passage about the economics professor and her\nhairdresser 锁定两位具体人物，要在原文里抓他们各自的具体说法，属于事实型细节题。\n【拆句】句子主干是 What do we learn from the passage about ...，about 引出涉及的对象；并\n列结构 the economics professor and her hairdresser 由 and 连接两个名词短语，无从\n句、无否定，结构平直无陷阱。\n【定位】The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser\nagreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.\n经济学家说她打算继续工作，因为她想这样做。理发师也同意继续工作，但理由不同：她需\n要钱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe hairdresser agreed but for a different reason ↔\nThey differ in their reasons for continuing to work\n预判强调 \"两人继续工作的理由不同\"，A 项 They differ in their reasons for\ncontinuing to work 恰好对此点 100% 命中，无引申无超纲，可直接锁定。\nB) 答非所问\nB 项 They are both committed to working into their 70s 把题目焦点偏到 \"是否都坚\n持工作到 70 多岁\"，但题目问的是 \"对照两人有何不同\"，不是问共同点。\nC) 无中生有\nC 项 They are happy about their current financial situation 说两人都对当前财务状\n况满意，但理发师明确说 She needs the money，怎么也谈不上 happy。\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 They hold different views about postponing retirement 似乎与 A 相近，但\ndifferent views about postponing retirement 意为 \"在是否推迟退休这件事上观点不\n同\"，而原文里两人都决定继续工作，差异在理由而不在观点。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is one of the reasons for people to delay retirement?", "options": ["A) More and more people have switched to less intellectually challenging jobs.", "B) The number of jobs suitable for older people has increased in the private sector.", "C) The rate of wage increase in terms of purchasing power has slowed down for years.", "D) More and more people in the workplace find it hard to rely on traditional pensions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is one of the reasons for people to delay retirement 询问具体原因，要在原\n文清单里点对清单项，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What is one of the reasons，后接介词短语 for people to delay retirement 作后置\n定语；for 引导原因方向，不定式 to delay 表逻辑动作，结构清晰，无难点。\n【定位】In addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a\ncombination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a\nshift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people\ndelay retirement.\n除了人们更健康、寿命更长之外，经济学家说，多重经济因素的叠加——例如实际工资多年\n增长缓慢，以及私人部门远离传统养老金——是人们延迟退休的部分原因。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文说 the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive\noccupations——是 \"从事劳动强度小的职业的人变多\"，A 项 less intellectually\nchallenging jobs 偷换为 \"智力上挑战较小的工作\"，labor-intensive（体力密集）与\nintellectually challenging（智力挑战）不是一回事。\nB) 无中生有\nB 项 The number of jobs suitable for older people has increased in the private\nsector 说私人部门里适合老年人的岗位增多，但原文 a shift away from traditional\npensions in the private sector 谈的是养老金机制远离传统模式，并未谈岗位数量的\n变化。\nC) ✓ 正确\nyears of slow rise in real wages ↔\nThe rate of wage increase in terms of purchasing power has slowed down for years\n预判锁定 \"实际工资多年涨得慢\"，C 项 The rate of wage increase in terms of\npurchasing power has slowed down for years 把同一信息用购买力的角度表达，是\n教科书式同义替换。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector 是 \"私人部门正在\n远离传统养老金\"——指制度变化；D 项 find it hard to rely on traditional pensions\n偷换成 \"人们觉得难以依赖传统养老金\"——指主观感受。两者方向虽近，但原文从未谈\n及人们的主观难度感。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the general trend in people delaying retirement?", "options": ["A) The higher their earnings, the more likely they are tempted to delay retirement.", "B) Those who have more job satisfaction tend to retire later than those who have less.", "C) More men than women are likely to stay in the labor force until their late seventies.", "D) The higher their educational level, the more likely they are to delay retirement."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the general trend in people delaying retirement 询问的是原文里已经写明\n的整体规律，去找总括句即可，不需越级推断。\n【拆句】主干 What is the general trend，介词短语 in people delaying retirement 中 people\ndelaying retirement 是 in 的复合宾语（动名词短语带逻辑主语），结构平直、无从句。\n【定位】The overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment,\nalthough the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor's degrees\nwho were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school\ndegree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while\nthose with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.\n这一整体趋势影响着所有学历层次的美国人，尽管各类别的比例有所不同。拥有本科学历、\n且仍在 70 多岁工作的美国人比例在 2018 年达到近 20%；高中及以下学历者比例约\n10%；有部分大学经历者居中，约 15%。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nA 项 The higher their earnings, the more likely they are tempted to delay\nretirement 把维度换成 earnings（收入），原文 all different levels of educational\nattainment 谈的是学历，没有任何收入分层数据。\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 项 Those who have more job satisfaction tend to retire later than those who\nhave less 谈职业满意度的影响，但本段只有学历分层数据，没有关于 job satisfaction\n的对比；此话题虽在第 6 段简短出现，与 \"general trend\" 不是同一议题。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 项 More men than women are likely to stay in the labor force until their late\nseventies 涉及性别差异，原文虽然第 6 段提到 particularly for men，但那是讲孤独\n感、不是 general trend 数据；本段从未做性别对比。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe share of Americans with bachelor's degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly\n20%\n↔ The higher their educational level, the more likely they are to delay retirement\n预判出 \"学历越高，延迟退休比例越高\"，D 项 The higher their educational level, the\nmore likely they are to delay retirement 正是这一规律的语句化，无遗漏无超纲。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the chief reason for college-educated workers delaying retirement according to an economist?", "options": ["A) Enjoying financial security.", "B) Staying connected socially.", "C) Contributing more professionally.", "D) Increasing social security payments."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the chief reason for college-educated workers delaying retirement 询问\n原文中经济学家明确给出的主因，按细节题处理即可。\n【拆句】主干 What is the chief reason，介词短语 for college-educated workers delaying\nretirement 作后置定语，分词 delaying 修饰 workers 表正在延迟；末尾 according to an\neconomist 是插入限定信息源，结构清晰。\n【定位】He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more\nfor social benefits than for financial reasons.\n他指出，许多受过大学教育的劳动者选择继续留在劳动力市场，更多是出于社交方面的好\n处，而不是经济原因。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nA 项 Enjoying financial security 把主因说成 \"享受经济保障\"，但原文明确 more for\nsocial benefits than for financial reasons —— financial 恰恰是被否定的次要原因，\nA 项正好反向。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmore for social benefits than for financial reasons ↔ Staying connected socially\n预判抓 social benefits，B 项 Staying connected socially 正面命中；其余选项均与\nsocial 无直接对应，可一眼锁定。\nC) 过度推断\nC 项 Contributing more professionally 说 \"在专业上贡献更多\"，原文 in professional\noccupations where they tend to enjoy their work more 只说他们多从事专业职业、\n并享受工作，并未提 \"为了贡献更多\" 才不退休。\nD) 无中生有\nD 项 Increasing social security payments 把 social 与 financial 拼成 \"提高社会保障\n金\"，是基于熟词 social 的乱凑，原文从未提及社会保障金（social security 是美国福利\n专名）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the passage say about people doing manual work?", "options": ["A) They are eager to enjoy life after retiring.", "B) They are likely to have financial troubles.", "C) They generally don't enjoy doing it.", "D) They tend to anticipate retirement."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about people doing manual work 直接问原文对该群\n体的具体说法，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】主干 What does the passage say about ...，about 引出对象；现在分词短语 doing\nmanual work 后置修饰 people，相当于 who do manual work，无从句无难点。\n【定位】Workers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring,\nBaily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.\n另一方面，从事体力劳动较多的工人则可能更期待退休；Baily 说，那些继续留下来的多半\n是出于经济原因。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nA 项 They are eager to enjoy life after retiring 把原文 \"期待退休\" 引申到 \"渴望享受\n退休后的生活\"，原文只说期待退休、未提退休后做什么，引申了一步。\nB) 过度推断\nB 项 They are likely to have financial troubles 把原文 those who stay on are more\nlikely doing so for financial reasons 推到 \"会陷入经济困境\"，但 for financial\nreasons 只说 \"出于经济原因\"，不等同于 financial troubles。\nC) 无中生有\nC 项 They generally don't enjoy doing it 说 \"他们通常不喜欢做这工作\"，但原文只\n说他们更期待退休、并未直接评价是否喜欢工作；不能由 \"期待退休\" 直接等同 \"不喜欢\n工作\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmay be more likely to look forward to retiring ↔ They tend to anticipate retirement\n预判抓 \"期待退休\"，D 项 They tend to anticipate retirement 是 look forward to\nretiring 的标准英英同义替换，选定无误。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
